].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366253 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366254 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366255 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366256 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186366257 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1041 "selection" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1104 #edb/ref 17592186366255 13194139535786 true] [17592186366252 1110 #uuid "93da46da-c2e7-5e08-900a-46d5f09a262e" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366253 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535786 true] [17592186366253 1111 #uuid "bca98281-a78b-5897-8882-67fd6710fbaa" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366254 1034 "I only" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535786 true] [17592186366254 1111 #uuid "10df0dd1-279a-5d23-a482-111282e7cd0a" 13194139535786 true] [17592186366255 1034 "III only" 1319